(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-specific-immunity-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Acquisition of Specific Immunity — Quiz 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What happens to lymphocyte clones that develop a specificity for SELF?","choices":["They are eliminated in the fetus","They are retained as memory cells","They migrate to the thymus to mature","They are converted into plasma cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A defect at this point gives an inherited autoimmune disorder such as severe combined immunodeficiency. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which responses should an effective vaccine stimulate?","choices":["Cell-mediated immunity only","Innate immunity only","BOTH antibody and cell-mediated","Antibody-mediated immunity only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it should produce memory B and T cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Why does the marrow RECIPIENT receive drug and radiation therapy beforehand?","choices":["To reduce the risk of rejecting the donor cells","To destroy the donor cells' antigens","To raise the recipient's own stem cell count","To prevent infection during transport"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — donor and recipient preparation are closely coordinated. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which antibodies are produced in the PRIMARY response?","choices":["IgG only, in very large titre","IgA only","IgE only","IgM and IgG"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, along with memory B cells specific for that antigen. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Early passive immunotherapy used horse serum. What are its risks, and is it still used?","choices":["No risks, and it is obsolete","Only local reactions, and it is obsolete","Cytokine storm, and it is obsolete","Serum sickness or anaphylaxis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. And yes, still sometimes, for diphtheria, botulism and bites. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is the LEAST common in serum and the shortest-lived?","choices":["IgM","IgA","IgE","IgD"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it appears mainly against allergens and parasitic worms. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which cells does IgE bind, and what does that produce?","choices":["B cells — triggering their activation","Neutrophils — driving phagocytosis","Erythrocytes — causing agglutination","Mast cells and basophils"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It mediates asthma and anaphylaxis, usually through histamine from the mast cells. Stimulating the inflammatory response. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Bee or wasp venom belongs in which allergen category?","choices":["Inhaled","Ingested","Contact","Injected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside some medications. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What happens to the genes encoding the variable region once a B cell is committed?","choices":["They are replaced by the antigen's own genes","They are locked in for the life of the cell and its progeny","They continue to rearrange with each division","They are discarded after the first antigen encounter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — including its memory cells and activated descendants. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin type is the first receptor on a YOUNG B cell, and which on a mature one?","choices":["IgG on young cells; IgA on mature cells","IgD on young cells; IgM on mature cells","IgE on young cells; IgG on mature cells","A small version of IgM on young cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. IgD on mature cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What makes the SECONDARY response faster and stronger?","choices":["Innate immunity taking over","Complement being activated sooner","Memory cells","A larger dose of antigen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They produce a much higher titre of IgG, followed by gradual IgM. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the Major Histocompatibility Complex also known as?","choices":["Immunoglobulin","Human leukocyte antigen","Cluster of differentiation","Antigenic determinant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — MHC and HLA are the same thing. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which MHC class is found on macrophages, antigen-presenting cells and B lymphocytes?","choices":["Class II","Class I","Class III","Class IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the immune regulatory receptors. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does an APC do to the antigen before presenting it?","choices":["Binds it to circulating antibody","Attaches it to class II MHC","Secretes it unchanged","Attaches it to a class I MHC receptor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How large is the naive lymphocyte pool, theoretically?","choices":["About a million variations","Exactly as many as there are antigens","Up to a quintillion variations","About a thousand variations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — up to a quintillion. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is particularly effective at AGGLUTINATION, and why?","choices":["IgG, because it is the most prevalent","IgE, because it binds mast cells","IgD, because it sits on B cells","IgM — ten binding sites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cross-linking adjacent bacterial cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Responses that stimulate T cells are called what?","choices":["Passive immunity","Cell-mediated immunity","Antibody-mediated immunity","Humoral-mediated immunity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Where do all leukocytes originate?","choices":["The bone marrow","The thymus","The spleen","The liver"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and there are five distinct categories. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Responses that stimulate B cells are called what?","choices":["Innate immunity","Species immunity","Antibody-mediated immunity","Cell-mediated immunity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — AMI, or HMI in older textbooks. Or humoral-mediated immunity. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does interleukin 2 do once the T helper cell is activated?","choices":["Activates B cells and other T cells","Triggers fever","Opsonizes bacteria","Lyses virally infected cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dysregulation of it can contribute to lupus and rheumatoid arthritis. Enhances T helper function. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is a HAPTEN, and how can it become antigenic?","choices":["A molecule under 1,000 molecular weight","A molecule over 100,000 molecular weight — antigenic on its own","A fragment of antibody — antigenic when secreted","A self molecule — antigenic when tolerance fails"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Drugs, metals and industrial chemicals behave this way, which is how occupational allergies to latex or cleaning chemicals arise. Antigenic only when attached to a larger carrier. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is an antigenic determinant also called, and what is it?","choices":["An epitope — a small molecular group recognized by lymphocytes","A hapten — a small molecule that is not antigenic alone","An adjuvant — a substance that boosts a response","A titre — a measure of antibody concentration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and one antigen may carry many of them. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What must an antigen-presenting cell use to bring bound antigen to a T helper cell?","choices":["An immunoglobulin","A complement receptor","A class II MHC receptor","A class I MHC receptor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is required for the APC to activate the T helper cell. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In which form or forms does IgA occur?","choices":["Tetramer only","Monomer or dimer","Pentamer only","Monomer only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, including a free or secretory form. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which three cells collaborate directly in antigen presentation?","choices":["Two B cells and one T cell","An APC, a T helper cell","A neutrophil, a basophil, and a mast cell","A plasma cell, a memory cell, and a stromal cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. T-cell dependent antigens must be processed by an antigen-presenting cell first. An antigen-specific B or T cell. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What distinguishes SPECIFIC immunity from innate immunity?","choices":["It is adaptive","It is present from birth","It works only against bacteria","It requires no lymphocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Innate immunity is what we are born with; specific immunity has to be acquired. Acquired through contact with infectious agents. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which antibody dominates the secondary response, and which follows it?","choices":["IgD alone","A much higher titre of IgG","IgM first, then IgG","IgA first, then IgE"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the reverse of the primary response's order of prominence. Followed by gradual IgM. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How does antibody lead to complement-mediated lysis?","choices":["It digests the cell envelope itself","It binds the bacterium","It converts directly into complement proteins","It blocks complement from reaching the cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is complement fixation in the specific immune response. Leaving sites for complement proteins to bind and use perforins. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What two cell types arise from B-cell clonal expansion?","choices":["Cytotoxic and helper T cells","Macrophages and dendritic cells","Stromal cells and stem cells","Plasma cells that secrete antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does antibody NEUTRALIZE a virus?","choices":["By digesting viral nucleic acid","By cross-linking virions into clumps","By blocking its attachment spikes","By lysing the viral envelope directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it also makes the virus easier to phagocytose. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
